.4 How much smaller than the product bound","+","+`log[C(N, t)(1−p)^{t(N−t)}] − log P_N(E_bip)`, total nats and per vertex of","+`N` (`N` even throughout, so `ln 2 = 0.693` of it is the double count):","+","+| `c′` | `N = 20` | 40 | 60 | 80 | 100 | 120 | per vertex at 120 | growth per vertex, 60 → 120 |","+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|","+| 2 | 3.74 | 7.99 | 12.41 | 16.89 | 21.41 | 25.96 | 0.216 | 0.226 |","+| 3 | 2.03 | 4.11 | 6.39 | 8.75 | 11.15 | 13.60 | 0.113 | 0.120 |","+| 4 | 1.23 | 2.23 | 3.40 | 4.65 | 5.96 | 7.29 | 0.061 | 0.065 |","+| 5 | 0.89 | 1.35 | 1.93 | 2.58 | 3.28 | 4.00 | 0.033 | 0.035 |","+| 6.6 | 0.72 | 0.84 | 1.01 | 1.22 | 1.45 | 1.70 | 0.014 | 0.012 |","+| 8 | 0.70 | 0.73 | 0.78 | 0.84 | 0.92 | 1.01 | 0.008 | 0.004 |","+| 10 | 0.69 | 0.70 | 0.70 | 0.71 | 0.73 | 0.74 | 0.006 | 0.0007 |","+","+**At `c′ ≥ 8` the whole gain is the factor 2**: the gap is `ln 2` plus","+`0.01–0.3` nats at `N = 120` and grows at under `0.004` nats per vertex.","+What the separator count merges there is the two orientations of one","+bipartition and nothing else, as item 03 measured on typical instances","+(`X₁/X₂ = 2.0–2.4` with `N` even, §3.2) and as the LDP says in the limit","+(`I(½) − (c′/4 − ln 2) = 0.009` at `c′ = 8`, `0.003` at 10). **Below","+`c′ ≈ 5` the gap is linear in `N`** at `0.035, 0.065, 0.120, 0.226` nats","+per vertex of `N` for `c′ = 5, 4, 3, 2`, the limit values being","+`I(½) − max(0, c′/4 − ln 2) = 0.043, 0.073, 0.126, 0.036`","+(at `c′ = 2` the pair count's rate is negative, so the pair bound is","+vacuous and the gap per vertex is `ln 2 − c′/4` plus the rate: the","+finite table's `0.226` has the product bound still *growing*). This is","+the component structure doing real work: the bipartitions of `G(N, c′/N)`","+that are empty cluster onto the same component partition, and the","+smaller `c′` the more of them per partition.","+","+In the loop's units (nats per vertex of `n`, then `β`): at a pilot cell","+`N = (1 − β) n`, so the gain per vertex of `n` is `(1 − β)` times the","+column above at `c′ = c(1 − β)`. Linearising the pair exponent at `β*`","+with the LDP value of the gain gives `+0.023, +0.020, +0.011, +0.006,","++0.004` in `β` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10` — the same numbers as §1.6's","+preview from the quotation (`+0.028, +0.021, +0.011, +0.004` at","+`c = 3, 4, 6, 10`, by the exact root rather than the linearisation), now","+resting on the exact finite computation at `N ≤ 120` and the fitted","+rate beyond. Item 06 computes `β_sep(c)` properly; this is the preview","+it inherits.","+","+### 5.5 Kill verdict","+","+**Met for the headline cells.** At every pilot cell with `c ≥ 6` the","+separator floor at `< 0.01` exceeds the pair floor by at most one stack","+(`+0, +0, +1, +1, +0, +1`), so **the separator count is recorded as a","+rounding at these sizes**, as the exact-size pair was in","+`excess_phase_diagram.md` §8.6 (4), and item 06 is told so. The rate","+comparison ran as the item requires, and is where the item's value is:","+§5.3 (1)–(2) settle the two open readings of item 01, and §5.4 locates","+the gain on the sparse flank (`c′ ≤ 5`, i.e. `c ≤ 6` at `β*`), two to","+three stacks at `n = 120`, `c = 3–4`.","+","+**Prediction for item 06**: `β_sep(c) = β_relax(c)` of §1.6 to the","+accuracy of the fit (the two events have the same rate to `N = 120` and","+to three decimals in the fit); gains of `+0.011` at `c = 6` and `+0.004`","+at 10 against the kill of `0.02`, so **item 06's kill is predicted met","+at `c = 6` and `10`**, and not met at `c ≤ 4`. The prediction of §3.5","+(the separator count merges at most `0.003` in `β` at `c = 10`, measured","+at `k = pw` on typical instances) is consistent with the `0.004` at `β*`.","+","+### 5.6 What this does not claim","+","+* **A limit.** The rate of `E_bip` is computed exactly to `N = 120` and","+  fitted beyond; the rate of `E_relax` is exact to `N = 400` and fitted","+  beyond. That either equals `I_{c′}(½)` is a fit agreeing to three","+